Let $\Lambda=\operatorname{End}_\Gamma(T)$. It is given by the right-hand quiver
in Figure~\ref{fig:e6}, with relations
\[
\alpha\gamma=\beta\delta=0,\qquad
	\gamma\rho=\delta\sigma=:c.
\]
The classical results in
\cite[Chapter~III]{Happel} yield
$\mathrm D^b(\operatorname{mod} \Lambda)\simeq\mathrm D^b(\operatorname{mod} \Gamma)$.
We claim there is a two-term silting complex $\mathbf P\in \mathrm K^{\mathrm b}(\operatorname{proj} \Lambda)$ with 
\[
I_1=(\gamma),\qquad I_2=(\gamma,\delta)=I_3,
\] and thus $h_{\Lambda}(\mathbf P)=2$.

Write $P_i=e_i\Lambda$ and $S_i$ the corresponding simple $\Lambda$-modules. Take $\mathbf P$ to be the direct sum of the following summands:
\[
\begin{gathered}
	\mathbf P_{\mathrm{I}}=[P_2\xrightarrow{\alpha}P_1],\quad
		\mathbf P_{\mathrm{II}}=[P_2\xrightarrow{\beta}P_4],\quad
	\mathbf P_{\mathrm{III}}=[P_{2}\xrightarrow{(\alpha,-\beta)^t}P_{1}\oplus P_{4}],\\
	\mathbf P_{\mathrm{IV}}=P_{3}[0],\quad
		\mathbf P_{\mathrm{V}}=[P_{6}\xrightarrow{(\rho,-\sigma)^t}P_{3}\oplus P_{5}],\quad
		\mathbf P_{\mathrm{VI}}=[P_{6}\xrightarrow{\rho}P_{3}].
\end{gathered}
\]

By
\cite[Lemma~2.5, Lemma~2.2(2)]{HKM} with $n=1$, we have
\[
\operatorname{Hom}(\mathbf P,\mathbf P[1])\simeq\operatorname{Hom}(\mathbf P,H^0(\mathbf P)[1])\simeq
\operatorname{coker}\bigl(
\operatorname{Hom}_\Lambda(P^0,H^0(\mathbf P))\xrightarrow{(-)\circ d_{\mathbf P}}
\operatorname{Hom}_\Lambda(P^{-1},H^0(\mathbf P))
\bigr).
\]
It can be checked that the map $\operatorname{Hom}_\Lambda(P^0,H^0(\mathbf P))\xrightarrow{(-)\circ d_{\mathbf P}}
\operatorname{Hom}_\Lambda(P^{-1},H^0(\mathbf P))$ on each source summand of $\mathbf P$ and target
summand of $H^0(\mathbf P)$ is surjective. Therefore
$\operatorname{Hom}(\mathbf P,\mathbf P[1])=0$ and $\mathbf P$ is presilting.

Moreover, there is
a distinguished triangle
\[
\Lambda\longrightarrow\mathbf P_{\mathrm{III}}^{3}\oplus \mathbf P_{\mathrm{IV}}^{2}\oplus \mathbf P_{\mathrm{V}}
\longrightarrow \mathbf P_{\mathrm{I}}^{2}\oplus \mathbf P_{\mathrm{II}}^{2}\oplus \mathbf P_{\mathrm{VI}}^{2}
\longrightarrow \Lambda[1].
\]
It follows that 
$\Lambda \in\operatorname{thick}(\mathbf P)$. Consequently
$\operatorname{thick}(\mathbf P)=\mathrm K^{\mathrm b}(\operatorname{proj} \Lambda)$, and $\mathbf P$ is silting.

By Lemma~\ref{lem:hom-id}, the negative chain maps correspond to
$\operatorname{Hom}_\Lambda(H^0(\mathbf{P}),H^{-1}(\mathbf{P}))$.
Their matrix entries generate $I_1$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:matrix-hrs-ideal}.
The three nonzero kernels are
\[
H^{-1}(\mathbf{P}_{\mathrm{I}})\cong P_3,\qquad
H^{-1}(\mathbf{P}_{\mathrm{II}})\cong P_5,\qquad
H^{-1}(\mathbf{P}_{\mathrm{III}})\cong S_6.
\]
The remaining three summands have zero kernel.
Comparing $H^0(\mathbf{P})$ with these three modules gives
$\operatorname{Hom}_\Lambda(H^0(\mathbf P),H^{-1}(\mathbf P))\simeq k,$
generated by the map $H^0(\mathbf{P}_{\mathrm{IV}})\to H^{-1}(\mathbf{P}_{\mathrm{I}})$
whose corresponding negative chain map has coefficient $\gamma$.
Lemma~\ref{lem:matrix-hrs-ideal} gives
\[
I_1=(\gamma)=\langle\gamma,\beta\gamma,\gamma\rho\rangle_k.
\]

For $R_1=H^{-1}(\mathbf{P})\cap P^{-1}I_1$, a direct calculation gives
\[R_1=\gamma \Lambda\oplus k(\gamma\rho)\oplus k(\gamma\rho).\]
Hence
\[H^{-1}(\mathbf P)/R_1\simeq\delta \Lambda/k(\gamma\rho)\simeq S_5.\]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:main-kernel-filtration},
$\operatorname{Hom}(\mathbf P_1,\mathbf P_1[-1])\simeq k$.
Its generator is represented by
\[
L=(0,\bar\delta): \mathbf{P}_{\mathrm{V}}^0\longrightarrow\mathbf{P}_{\mathrm{II}}^{-1}
\quad\text{over }\Lambda/I_1.
\]
Indeed, $\beta\delta=0$ and
$L d_{\mathbf P_{\mathrm{V}}}=-\overline{\delta\sigma}=0$
over $\Lambda/I_1$.
Since every negative chain map $\mathbf{P}\to\mathbf{P}[-1]$ over $\Lambda$ is a scalar
multiple of the map with coefficient $\gamma$, all such maps reduce to zero
modulo $I_1$.
The nonzero map $L$ therefore does not lift to a negative chain map over
$\Lambda$.
Lemma~\ref{lem:matrix-hrs-ideal} now gives
$I_2/I_1=\langle\bar\delta,\overline{\alpha\delta}\rangle_k$.
Taking its inverse image under $\Lambda\twoheadrightarrow\Lambda/I_1$
gives $I_2=(\gamma,\delta)$.

Since $e_2I_2=\langle\gamma,\delta,c\rangle_k$, each nonzero kernel lies
in the $I_2$-multiple of its corresponding $P_2$-summand.
Hence $R_2=H^{-1}(\mathbf P)$.
Theorem~\ref{thm:main-kernel-filtration} yields
$I_3=I_2$ and $h_\Lambda(\mathbf P)=2$.
Therefore
\[
0=I_0\subsetneq I_1\subsetneq I_2=I_3,
\quad h_{\mathrm{HRS}}(\Lambda)\ge h_\Lambda(\mathbf P)=2.
\]
